#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073507 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073741 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fdb1e0fe-da89-5d80-b9a0-82d4daddc396" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Missing Angle in a Triangle"}] :question/id #uuid "ed0361cf-cec0-5ec2-baab-e0f558619191" :question/math-academy-id "e-14725" :question/problem "![](images/1a/1afba9a36c6fd04fecfd50877181380776e13a83422697de4a39d71381f09aff.png)\n\n \n\nFind the value of $x$ in the triangle above." :question/worked-solution "Since the sum of the measures of the interior angles in any triangle is equal to $180^\\circ$, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠A+m∠B+m∠C & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ {36}^{∘}+{112}^{∘}+x & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ {148}^{∘}+x & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ x & ={32}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073508 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073742 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3eb85b52-cd40-5469-b2f5-ceee2f0bf0cb" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Unknown Quantity From a Triangle"}] :question/id #uuid "bf72c879-105c-5416-8dbd-7976228f0b4b" :question/math-academy-id "e-14727" :question/problem "![](images/e4/e43daa39b169c8ba29c21dd33e7a7fab1e92c7c78eafbd1021971e1883f6c9f8.png)\n\n \n\nFind the measure of $\\angle C.$" :question/worked-solution "Since the sum of the measures of the interior angles of a triangle is $180^\\circ$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3z+4z+{40}^{∘} & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ 7z+{40}^{∘} & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ 7z & ={140}^{∘} \\\\ z & ={20}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠C & =4z \\\\  & =4({20}^{∘}) \\\\  & ={80}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073509 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073743 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6a4f969d-aebf-53b5-ad47-1376bb98dc6a" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Unknown Quantity Given a Description of a Triangle"}] :question/id #uuid "8200fc85-afcc-5fb9-b942-cedbc87c739b" :question/math-academy-id "e-14728" :question/problem "Consider a triangle $\\triangle ABC$, where $m\\angle{A} = 55^\\circ$, $m\\angle B = x + 10^\\circ$, and $m\\angle C = 3x - 5^\\circ.$ Find the value of $x.$" :question/worked-solution "The sum of the measures of the interior angles must be $180^\\circ.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{55}^{∘}+(x+{10}^{∘})+(3x-{5}^{∘}) & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ {60}^{∘}+4x & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ 4x & ={120}^{∘} \\\\ x & ={30}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073510 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073744 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8b1f86d8-805c-55c7-8e8c-d96d99e3636b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Angles in Triangles With Congruent Angles"}] :question/id #uuid "b015f8d6-ac1b-537d-a673-839fc7418f71" :question/math-academy-id "e-15572" :question/problem "![](images/1a/1a24187f280eb99640c3d1bfae3b7fe873383f04c2c492e7873a40de16a9560b.png)\n\nFind the measure of angle $C$ given that $m\\angle{A} = m\\angle{B}.$" :question/worked-solution "Using $m\\angle{A} = m\\angle{B},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠A & =m∠B \\\\ 3x-{27}^{∘} & =2x \\\\ x & ={27}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, substituting $x=27^\\circ$ in the expression for $m\\angle B,$ we get\n\n$$\nm\\angle{B}=2(27^\\circ)=54^\\circ.\n$$\n\nNow, we can find $m\\angle C$ using the fact that the internal angles of any triangle sum to $180^\\circ$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠A+m∠B+m∠C & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ 2m∠B+m∠C & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ 2\\cdot {54}^{∘}+m∠C & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ {108}^{∘}+m∠C & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠C & ={72}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186488235 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073742 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3eb85b52-cd40-5469-b2f5-ceee2f0bf0cb" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Unknown Quantity From a Triangle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2d803c04-105c-51d2-a746-10efb22d5569" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{53}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 1759218648